/** @page identifiers Identifiers

    %Qore identifiers must start with an alphabetic character or an "_", and then may contain any number of alphabetic, numeric, or "_" characters. There is no length limit on %Qore identifiers.

    All %Qore identifiers are case-sensitive, therefore the identifier \c hello_there is not the same as \c Hello_There or \c HELLO_THERE.

    The following are examples of valid %Qore identifiers:

    <b>Examples of Valid %Qore Identifiers</b>
    |!Identifier|!Description
    |\c i|Simple one-character identifier
    |\c _|Another simple one-character identifier
    |\c foo21|Identifier with number
    |\c this_is_a_long_identifier|Long identifier with underline characters
    |\c _Total_318|Identifier with underlines and number
    |\c AVeryBigNumber|Mixed case identifier name
    |\c CAPS|Identifier in all capital letters

    The following are invalid identifiers:

    <b>Examples of Invalid %Qore Identifiers</b>
    |!Identifier|!Description
    |\c 1a|Does not start with an alphabetic character
    |\c this-and-that|Contains "-" characters
    |\c Start#10|Contains "#" character

    @section identifier_suggestions "Did You Mean ...?" Suggestions

    When an identifier cannot be resolved at parse time, %Qore looks for a closely
    matching name that is actually visible at the point of the error and, if it
    finds one, appends a <em>"did you mean ...?"</em> suggestion to the error
    message. This applies across the identifier surface: unresolved barewords,
    undefined classes, types, functions, constants, namespaces, hashdecl members,
    methods/members, and named arguments. Suggestions are always names that are
    legally usable in the current scope (respecting visibility), so they can be
    trusted by both humans and tooling.

    A simple typo of an existing function name:

    @code
int sub calculateTotal(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

int result = calculateTotl(2, 3);
    @endcode

    @verbatim
PARSE-EXCEPTION: function 'calculateTotl()' cannot be found; did you mean 'calculateTotal'?
    @endverbatim

    A difference only in capitalization is flagged specially, since %Qore
    identifiers are case-sensitive (see above):

    @code
class Widget {}
Widget w = new widget();
    @endcode

    @verbatim
PARSE-EXCEPTION: reference to undefined class 'widget'; did you mean 'Widget'? (check capitalization)
    @endverbatim

    When no name is close enough, no suggestion is offered (an arbitrary unknown
    name produces the plain "cannot be found" / "undefined" error). These
    suggestions are part of the normal error text and are also available in
    machine-readable form via the @ref diag_json_format "structured diagnostics"
    facility.
    @since %Qore 3.0
*/
